              {
                "prior_feedback_reference": "Finding 1: \"Since the summary is already in place, write the direct rule and keep the contrapositive one\" — rule_deposit_overflow_rejected_and_balance_untouched asserting r.is_err() and state.balance == before.",
                "evidence_type": "prover_output",
                "evidence": "That exact rule was in the draft for prover run #2 and came back ERROR, not VIOLATED, with no verdict and no vacuity verdict:\n\n  rule_deposit_rejects_balance_overflow result was: ERROR\n  [3308] illegal dereference of an absolute address\n  source: .../core/src/intrinsics.rs:2978\n    from: .../alloc/src/vec/mod.rs:2147\n    from: .../alloc/src/vec/spec_extend.rs:55\n    from: .../alloc/src/vec/mod.rs:2591\n    from: .../alloc/src/string.rs:1065\n    from: .../alloc/src/string.rs:2917\n    from: .../core/src/fmt/mod.rs:1627\n    from: programs/vault/src/lib.rs:168\n    from: .../alloc/src/string.rs:2558\n    from: programs/vault/src/lib.rs:168\n    from: .../core/src/result.rs:1964\n    from: programs/vault/src/lib.rs:94\n    from: programs/vault/src/certora/specs/deposits.rs:145\n(lib.rs:168 is the `#[error_code] pub enum VaultError` block; lib.rs:94 is the `ok_or(VaultError::Overflow)?` in `apply_deposit`.)\n\nThe summary is NOT 'already in place'. Every run since, including the one that stamped this draft, ends with:\n\n  1 of your 1 summary directive(s) match no symbol in this build, so they had no effect:\n    ^<vault::VaultError as core::fmt::Display>::fmt.*$\n        this build does define: <&T as core::fmt::Display>::fmt\n        this build does define: <...StringError as core::fmt::Display>::fmt\n        this build does define: <anchor_lang::error::ErrorCode as core::fmt::Display>::fmt\n  A symbol absent from the build is usually one the compiler inlined away, and no spelling of it will match.\n\nThere is also no callee 'one level out' to aim at: every frame between `apply_deposit` and the faulting store is a debug-info inline record, not a symbol — the Prover's own list of Display::fmt symbols in this build contains neither VaultError's impl nor anything on that chain. Per the charter's three-directives budget I stopped rather than walking the chain.\n\nI have therefore kept the contrapositive rule and documented the uncovered clause in its doc comment. On the summary's justification text: `summarize_for_prover` refused to replace it ('^<vault::VaultError as core::fmt::Display>::fmt.*$ is already summarized') and no tool withdraws a directive, so the correction is carried in the publication commentary and in this rule's doc comment instead."
              },
              {
                "prior_feedback_reference": "Finding 5: \"'the project's inlining configuration, which I cannot edit' is wrong as stated: cvlr_inlining_package.txt is the package-owned layer ... Either try it, or rewrite the skip\" and \"certora/confs/cvlr_deposits.conf passes solana_summaries but no inlining file at all\".",
                "evidence_type": "crate_source",
                "evidence": "Accepted and the skip is rewritten: it now names the exact remedy (`#[inline] ^<vault::Deposit as anchor_lang::Accounts<vault::DepositBumps>>::try_accounts$` in programs/vault/src/certora/envs/cvlr_inlining_package.txt) and says precisely why I cannot apply it — `summarize_for_prover` writes points-to summaries (more abstraction, the opposite of what is needed), `code_editor`'s charter covers the program's own source, and `adjust_prover_config` moves only loop_iter and the solver portfolio. Nothing I hold writes an inlining directive.\n\nOn which inlining set was in force: programs/vault/Cargo.toml carries\n\n  [package.metadata.certora]\n  solana_inlining = [\"src/certora/envs/cvlr_inlining.txt\"]\n  solana_summaries = [\"src/certora/envs/cvlr_summaries.txt\"]\n\nso the inlining layer is wired in through package metadata rather than through the conf, and cvlr_inlining.txt does contain `#[inline(never)] ^.*anchor_lang.*$` together with the `#[inline]` exceptions for `Signer::try_from`, `Account<T>::try_from` and `try_deserialize`. That is corroborated by the measurements: rules built from the per-field conversions show `Account::<VaultState>::try_from` / `try_deserialize` frames, and the one built from the generated `try_accounts` shows none and returns a `bool` holding 2."
              }
